     kkeerrmmiitt ss [_a_b_d_f_h_i_l_m_t_x _b_a_u_d _l_i_n_e] _f_i_l_e ...
     kkeerrmmiitt rr [_b_d_f_h_i_l_t _b_a_u_d _l_i_n_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     kkeerrmmiitt transfers files to and from a remote system using the
     Kermit protocol (Columbia University, 1981-1984).  The first
     command-line argument is a single word whose first letter
     selects the mode -- cc, CC, ss, or rr -- and whose remaining
     letters are single-letter options, run together with no
     separating dashes and no spaces; each option that takes a
     value (ll, ee, bb) consumes the next command-line word, in the
     order its letter appears in the mode word.  For example
          kermit sfb file 9600
     sends _f_i_l_e with filename-case conversion disabled (ff), at
     9600 baud (bb).

     cc    Connect: turn the local terminal into a terminal
          emulator attached to the tty line named by ll (default
          /ddeevv/ttttyy3388, default speed 2400).  Typing the escape
          character (default ^, a literal caret) followed by one
          more character invokes a local command: cc or CC hangs up
          the modem and closes the connection, ss or SS suspends
          the connection without hanging up, and hh or HH prints a
          ``still here'' message.  Any other character after the
          escape character just rings the bell.

     CC    Same as cc, except the next command-line word (after any
          options and their values) is sent down the line,
          followed by a carriage return, before the terminal
          emulator starts.

     ss    Send: transmit each named _f_i_l_e to the remote Kermit.

     rr    Receive: wait for the remote Kermit to send one or more
          files and store them in the current directory.

     dd    Increase the debugging level by one (repeatable).

     ii    Image mode: transfer 8-bit data without the usual 7-bit
          masking and CR/LF translation.

     ff    Do not convert filename case (normally lower case is
          folded to upper on send and upper to lower on receive).

     ll    The next word is the tty line to use, instead of the
          default.

     bb    The next word is the line speed in baud, instead of the
          default.



